# Build context for both images (the context is the repository root).
#
# An allow-list, not a deny-list: everything is excluded, then exactly what the
# two Dockerfiles copy is let back in. A deny-list has to be right about every
# file anyone will ever create here; this one fails safe. In particular a
# database, an uploaded photograph, a .env with the session key in it or a
# 300 MB node_modules can never be sent to the Docker daemon or baked into a
# layer, wherever in the tree they turn up.
*

!backend/
!frontend/
!deploy/
deploy/*
!deploy/docker/
!deploy/caddy/
!LICENSE
!NOTICE.md

# ...and inside the parts let back in, the things that still must not travel.
**/.env
**/.env.*
!**/.env.example
**/*.db
**/*.db-wal
**/*.db-shm
**/*.db-journal
**/*.sqlite3
backend/media/
backend/media_store/
backend/ci_media/
**/node_modules/
frontend/dist/
**/.vite/
**/__pycache__/
**/*.py[cod]
**/.pytest_cache/
**/.coverage
**/htmlcov/
**/.venv/
backend/tests/
backend/README.md
backend/requirements-dev.txt
frontend/e2e/
**/Dockerfile
**/*.md
!NOTICE.md
